# How Lifecycle Rules Work
A rule can apply to all objects in a bucket or only those with a prefix (like logs/) or tags (like project=archive).

Lifecycle actions include:

Transition actions – Move objects between storage classes:

Example: After 30 days → move from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA.

Example: After 365 days → move to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.

Expiration actions – Permanently delete objects:

Example: Delete objects after 730 days.

Example: Delete incomplete multipart uploads after 7 days.


#

{
  "Rules": [
    {
      "ID": "Move-Logs-To-Glacier-And-Delete",
      "Filter": {
        "Prefix": "logs/"
      },
      "Status": "Enabled",
      "Transitions": [
        {
          "Days": 30,
          "StorageClass": "STANDARD_IA"
        },
        {
          "Days": 90,
          "StorageClass": "GLACIER"
        }
      ],
      "Expiration": {
        "Days": 365
      },
      "NoncurrentVersionExpiration": {
        "NoncurrentDays": 30
      }
    }
  ]
}



##
Here’s a compact but complete list of key AWS S3 points you should remember — useful for interviews, exams, or daily cloud work:

1. Basics
S3 = Simple Storage Service → Object storage, not block or file storage.

Global service → Bucket names are unique globally.

Objects consist of data + metadata + key (name).

Max object size = 5 TB (single upload max 5 GB; bigger needs Multipart Upload).

2. Storage Classes
S3 Standard – High availability, low latency.

S3 Intelligent-Tiering – Moves data automatically between tiers.

S3 Standard-IA – Infrequent Access, lower cost, retrieval fee.

S3 One Zone-IA – Cheaper, stored in one AZ.

S3 Glacier / Deep Archive – Archival, long retrieval times.

3. Bucket Policies & Access
Access is private by default.

Bucket policy – JSON-based, applies to all objects in the bucket.

IAM policy – Applies to users/roles.

ACLs – Legacy method for object-level access.

Block Public Access – Must be disabled to allow public objects.

4. Versioning
Keeps multiple versions of an object.

Once enabled, can’t be disabled, only suspended.

5. Encryption
SSE-S3 – Managed by AWS (AES-256).

SSE-KMS – AWS KMS keys for more control.

SSE-C – Customer-provided keys.

Client-side encryption – Encrypt before upload.

6. Data Management
Lifecycle Rules – Move or delete data automatically (e.g., Standard → Glacier).

Replication – Cross-Region (CRR) or Same-Region (SRR). Requires versioning.

Object Lock – WORM (Write Once Read Many) for compliance.

7. Performance & Limits
Unlimited storage.

Object upload/download performance can be increased with S3 Transfer Acceleration.

Prefix in object key affects performance.

8. Logging & Monitoring
Access Logs – Track requests to bucket.

CloudTrail – Track API calls.

CloudWatch Metrics – Monitor storage, requests, errors.

9. Event Notifications
Can trigger Lambda, SNS, or SQS on object events (upload, delete, etc.).

10. Security
Supports VPC Endpoints for private access without internet.

IAM best practice: least privilege.

MFA Delete for extra protection.
